{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-118387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/3ae609102d5545d3db2e035238f030e3ee1b58be8aba716224d5df967ca6ed5a.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-118387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{2x-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/37434a61262f829160fc2b19fcadb67745a024d589cd1ce318ca4ab90d6ab2d9.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-3-=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{(\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{x}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-118389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-118389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-49051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x{log}_{2}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}"] ["math" "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x{log}_{2}⁡2}"] ["math" "{ln}^{2}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-49054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "{e}^{x}{sin}^{2}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/c924791712d00c951d9718fa976a00f35b2b3deb35d83301431ddb55d22326de.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{2x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({e}^{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+4{e}^{4x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-49049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "2{e}^{x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-49049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{e}^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x+5}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-344820" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344820" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/4e343d2884532c006e83cd4c90e70a6cd9af9247194c8a7f8832ef8cbaf293b5.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-8x+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-211191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=\\frac{7}{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+3}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/be3108802cde61c1ffcc5fdf603e97255b4082541f20f628aedd1a01127c05b7.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x$ that is closest to the point $(3,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =-y+{y}^{2}+9 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-y+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y-1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-344831" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344831" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{x-2}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{5}{2})=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-211194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-17073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/f6abba797edf9bd83882c31c2c45729fdcdb40226bf95cc10f0f01c90c68b5f1.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-17073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $D(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${D}^{′}(x)$ becomes $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-17073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-17073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-17073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{8}{5}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-17073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/4108c44d3fac12cc38c0aab73ed1600f034ad88c1c50be91ea7bb29145ce4dd7.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3$ and the point $(3,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{|x-1|})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{5}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-5(\\frac{5}{2})+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-344839" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344839" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2$ and the point $P(5,2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(5,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(4)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-32+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-211282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-211282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-315645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x-1}+2$ and the point $P(4,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-315645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x-1}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{4x-1}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x-1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+4x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}-4\\,(2)+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-315645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-315645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-315645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{11}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+2}+1$ and the point $P(3,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+2}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2x+2}+1-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x+2})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+2x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(2)+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-344845" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344845" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2$ and the point $P(1,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(1)^{2}-4(1)+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-4+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62fe613124f4d89819448f7427f916bdd0f5f63ddca97768dd495c8f881bb84e"} {"question" "q-344846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "q-344846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/state.json" "file_sha256" "20c5c16eb92c30bb3604e7a78ca6bb23ea3d9a0c2b7da7c828efcf0e37ea5f6f"} {"question" "e-12596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/example-12596.json" "file_sha256" "e5878a03eb66c0a9b2dbc828d1401058659a58a81ef96a2a930f77cdcbc81658"} {"question" "e-12596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/example-12596.json" "file_sha256" "e5878a03eb66c0a9b2dbc828d1401058659a58a81ef96a2a930f77cdcbc81658"} {"question" "e-12597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/example-12597.json" "file_sha256" "62de42a15cc56d83b1aa942d00b01f28b999942161e7423182f6099a80ea020f"} {"question" "e-12597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/example-12597.json" "file_sha256" "62de42a15cc56d83b1aa942d00b01f28b999942161e7423182f6099a80ea020f"} {"question" "e-12606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/44ef8fc83ea0a70dcc16769ee6965727ec2c9584e97cc417e34184d633bbf876.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/example-12606.json" "file_sha256" "c0d8aabcb4f891ddeeeb1d5463ec4d2e0fdf17ec33c355c8f289676baebb4bd7"} {"question" "e-12606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-6x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=6x-6 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''(1) =6 > 0.$ Therefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(1)^{2}-6(1)+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/example-12606.json" "file_sha256" "c0d8aabcb4f891ddeeeb1d5463ec4d2e0fdf17ec33c355c8f289676baebb4bd7"}] "usage" {"e-12596" [] "e-12597" [] "e-12606" []} "basis" 2286 "retractions" [[17592186198007 :question/problem "The $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation\n$2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0$.\nWhat is the missing expression?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4218/Source/Images/8d6bc7c46e9b7137f43fa5394d60c422.png)"] [17592186198007 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x, y) &= (x - 0)^{2} + (y - 0)^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}))^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + \\ln^{2} (\\frac{1}{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 2x + 2\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot (1)/((\\frac{1}{x})) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}(\\ln 1 - \\ln x) &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 2 ln ⁡ x x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}\n$$"] [17592186198008 :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$."] [17592186198008 :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nand a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S(x) &= x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + \\left(x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 4x^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(0) &= (0)^{4} - 2(0)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{9}{4} \\\\\nS(-1) &= (-1)^{4} - 2(-1)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{5}{4} \\\\\nS(1) &= (1)^{4} - 2(1)^{2} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1$."] [17592186198011 :question/problem "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4218/Source/Images/f39063edc015aeb9229c52d9e7752375.png)"] [17592186198011 :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3} + 1 - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3})^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 6x + 9 + 2x^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} - 6x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 6x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that\n\n$$\nS''(1) =6 > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S$.\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3(1)^{2} - 6(1) + 12} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
